# What survives on a small team's rig: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers with cheap hardware and little data

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Most robot-learning papers are tested in simulation or on rigs that cost more than $20k; this is the case for {{rig_far}} of the {{papers}} papers we studied. We re-tested {{c_paper}} of their claims on a rig of about €{{rig_eur_round}}, an SO-101 arm with a Jetson Orin NX. For each claim the bar was fixed before the test, and the cheapest baseline was run first on the same data. {{c_paper_repro}} claims hold and {{c_paper_fail}} fail. The failures take three forms: methods too slow for the edge GPU, judges that lose to the length of the episode, and learned latents that lose to the commanded joints. Many of these failures trace back to our small, short-task data as much as to the methods themselves. The code, the data and every number in the paper are public.

## 1. Introduction

Of the {{papers}} papers in this study, {{rig_far}} were shown in simulation or on a rig above $20k, and only {{rig_lt5k}} on a rig under $5k like ours. A small team that wants to use one of these methods has two options: trust the paper, or re-test the claim on its own hardware, which is slow and costly. The question we ask is simple: does a claim still hold for a small team, with cheap hardware, little data and short tasks?

To answer it, we took the claims one at a time and re-tested each on a single cheap rig. Adaptive chunking is a good example. The paper chooses the length of each action chunk from the entropy of several sampled chunks, so that the robot runs long chunks while moving between places and short ones at the grasp. To make one such decision the method samples 20 chunks, and on our Jetson Orin this took {{ex_a_ours}}, while our real-time budget is {{ex_a_bar}}. The claim fails on our rig. This does not mean it fails on the hardware it was shown on.

Over all the claims, {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} hold and {{c_paper_fail}} fail. Several of the failures are not near misses: the method loses to a baseline with no learning in it. Section 5 looks at these baselines, and Section 6 groups the failures into three classes. Section 7 then asks, for each failure, how much of it comes from the method and how much from our data and setup.

## 2. Rig and data

The rig is an SO-101 arm (five joints and a gripper) with a top camera and a wrist camera. Inference runs on a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB, and training runs on rented cloud GPUs. The priced parts cost €{{rig_eur}} ({{rig_unpriced}} small parts are not priced yet), and all the cloud GPU time came to ${{cost}}.

We used four sets of data. The first is our own teleoperated demonstrations (`so101_bowls_all_trimmed`): {{tel_eps}} episodes and {{tel_frames}} frames at {{tel_fps}} fps, about {{tel_min}} minutes in total, on two short tasks where one bowl is placed into another. We split them into {{tel_train}} training and {{tel_val}} validation episodes, and used them to train and test the world models and the VLA policies. The second is a set of rollouts of our trained policies on the arm (`so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`): {{ro_eps}} episodes, {{ro_succ}} successes and {{ro_fail}} failures, labelled by the operator. We used them for the failure monitors. The third is ArmnetBench [@2607.24481], a public set of {{arm_n}} labelled SO-101 rollouts from {{arm_pol}} policies, which we used for the reward models and judges. The last is {{tim_eps}} recorded episodes with {{tim_b}} chunk boundaries, used for the chunking and latency tests.

This data has clear limits. All tasks are short, about {{tel_ep_s}} s per episode, and none needs several steps or memory, so we say nothing about long-horizon tasks. There is one task family, one arm and one room. The outcome labels come from a single operator and were not given blind.

## 3. What we tested and how

We chose papers from the parts of the stack our rig runs, and they fall into four kinds (Table 1). World models ({{f_wm}} papers) learn a latent from the camera to predict the next state or to plan; we test whether the latent predicts or plans better than a signal we get for free. Failure monitors and judges ({{f_mon}} papers) say whether an episode is failing or how far it has progressed; we test whether they catch failures better than a trivial signal. VLA policies ({{f_vla}} papers) follow an instruction on the arm; we test whether they succeed on our arm, over 10 trials judged by the operator. Chunking and edge inference ({{f_chunk}} papers) concern how a policy's action chunks are produced and joined; we test whether the joins are smoother and how long a chunk takes on the Orin.

Every test follows two rules. First, the bar is written from the paper and its code only, before any result is seen, because a bar written after the result tends to drift toward it. Second, before the method itself, we run the cheapest baseline of its kind on the same data. The baseline is built from what we already have for free: the last frame, the joint values we send to the arm, the length of the episode, or the method with its main idea switched off. If the method loses to this baseline, its bar hardly matters. Each claim then gets one of four verdicts: it holds (the bar is met), it fails (the bar is missed), it is not proven (the test ran but cannot decide), or it is not tested yet.

A failure can also be our own fault, so where we could we ran a control. TurboVLA is the clearest case. We believed its architecture made our port of the policy ignore its instruction. We fixed the bar first: if swapping the task moved the output by {{tv_bug}}, the bug was in our port; if by {{tv_data}}, the problem was the data. With the upstream trainer on our data, swapping the task moved the output by only {{tv_swap}}, while their released LIBERO checkpoint, run on our rig, moved it by {{tv_ctl}}. The problem was our data, not their architecture. We withdrew the claim, and it is not counted in this paper.

Table 1: The four kinds of papers, what we test in each, the cheapest baseline we build for it, and the verdicts.

| kind of paper | papers | what we test | cheapest baseline | verdicts |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| world models | {{f_wm}} | predict the next state; rank or plan actions | copy-last (next state = current state); the commanded joints or the actions alone | {{f_wm_v}} |
| failure monitors and judges | {{f_mon}} | catch failures; track progress | episode length; the same model with shuffled actions | {{f_mon_v}} |
| VLA policies | {{f_vla}} | succeed on our arm | none cheaper: a success rate fixed before 10 trials | {{f_vla_v}} |
| chunking and edge inference | {{f_chunk}} | smoother joins between chunks; time per chunk | the same policy without the trick; the real-time budget on the Orin | {{f_chunk_v}} |

## 4. Results

Figure 1 and Table 2 give the verdicts, and Table 1 breaks them down by kind of paper. Of the {{c_paper}} claims, {{c_paper_repro}} hold, {{c_paper_fail}} fail, {{c_not_proven}} are not proven and {{c_not_tested}} are not tested yet. Counted by paper, {{p_reproduces}} of the {{papers}} reproduce, {{p_fail}} fail, {{p_not_proven}} are not proven and {{p_not_tested}} is not tested. The pattern by kind is sharp: all {{f_mon}} failure monitors and judges fail, while all {{f_vla}} VLA policies reproduce. Section 7 shows that the first result is weaker evidence than it looks. The appendix gives every paper with its claim, its bar and our result.

