Table 2: Verdicts by paper and by claim.

| verdict | papers | claims |
|---|---|---|
| reproduces | {{p_reproduces}} | {{c_reproduces}} |
| fails | {{p_fail}} | {{c_fail}} |
| not proven | {{p_not_proven}} | {{c_not_proven}} |
| not tested | {{p_not_tested}} | {{c_not_tested}} |

## 5. The cheapest baseline

The cheapest baseline is the signal we get for free on the same data as the method (Table 1). It needs no training and runs in minutes. We compared {{b_n}} paper claims with such a baseline (Table 3). The method clearly beat it in {{b_beat}} cases, beat it by less than its bar in {{b_under}}, and lost to it in {{b_lost}}.

The choice of baseline decides the verdict, even for a single model. LeWorldModel predicts the next latent far better than copy-last, with an error of {{b_lewm_pred}} against {{b_lewm_cl}}, and that claim holds. Yet the same model ranks the chunk that was really executed worse than the actions alone do, {{ex_c_ours}} against {{ex_c_base}}, and that claim fails. Predicting well is not the same as planning well, and testing only one of the two would give the wrong picture.

The same baseline is also not equally strong on all data. On ArmnetBench, episode length beats the reward model ({{ex_b_len}} against {{ex_b_ours}}), because there a failed episode tends to run until the time limit. On our own 88 rollouts, where failures do not run to the time limit, length is close to chance ({{b_len_own}}). What a judge must beat therefore depends on who stopped the episode, and the baseline has to be computed on one's own data.

Copy-last and the joints are strong partly because our task is short and simple: little moves apart from the arm, and the arm's state is already in the joints. On long tasks with many moving objects a learned latent has more to add, and we did not test that. Some papers report a cheap baseline themselves (shuffled actions for ReAP, a random pick for PACT-VLA, net joint displacement for HWM), and on our data those methods beat it too. Robometer reports no episode-length baseline. In practice, the baseline is the first thing to run: if the method loses to it, one can stop there, and only if it wins is the bar worth checking.

Table 3: The paper claims we compared with a cheap baseline, on the same data.

| method | baseline (what is measured) | method | baseline | outcome |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | copy-last (latent error at 30 steps, lower is better) | 0.31 | 0.89 | beat |
| LeWorldModel | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.60 | 0.68–0.90 | lost |
| DINO-WM | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.61 | 0.68–0.90 | lost |
| RoboJEPA | copy-last (9-step latent error) | 11.8 % below copy-last | – | beat, under its bar of 15 % |
| ContactGuard | the current latent (failure AUROC) | 0.70 | 0.82 | lost |
| ReAP | shuffled actions (failure AUROC) | 0.789 | 0.708 | beat |
| Robometer | episode length (failure AUROC, ArmnetBench) | 0.818 | 0.938 | lost |
| HWM | net joint displacement (cosine to the macro-action) | 0.608 | 0.551 | beat |
| Adaptive chunking | fixed chunk length (boundary jump, lower is better) | 0.973 | 0.940 | lost |
| PACT-VLA | a random pick (error of the executed window) | −0.27° against random | – | beat |
| REMORA | per-frame readout (readout flips) | 0.70 × per-frame | – | beat, under its bar of 0.5 × |

## 6. Three failure classes

We see three classes in the failures. The first, class A, is the clock: the method does not fit the real-time budget of the edge GPU. Adaptive chunking needs {{ex_a_ours}} for one decision against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}. It may well work on a faster GPU, and this class is the easiest to avoid, since a method can be timed on the target device before anything else is built.

Class B concerns judges, the models that say whether an episode succeeds, fails or progresses. A 4B reward model used zero-shot on ArmnetBench reaches an AUROC of {{ex_b_ours}}, while episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}.

Class C concerns learned latents, the vectors a world model computes from the camera. Our world model ranks the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors against a bar of {{ex_c_bar}}, while a baseline using only the actions reaches {{ex_c_base}}. The commanded joints already hold the arm's state, so the latent has to add what the joints miss, such as where the objects are, and here it does not.

These classes are our proposal, based on the author's reading of the {{c_paper_fail}} failed claims, and a second reader would be needed to confirm them. They suggest an order for testing a paper on a cheap rig: time it on the device first, then compare it with the cheapest baseline, and only then look at its bar.

## 7. Why they failed here: the method, our data, or our setup

For each failure, Table 4 lists what on our side may explain it. Most failures have at least one such cause, so a failure here is a failure on this rig, with this data, and not a verdict on the method in general.

Our data is small and simple. We have {{tel_train}} training episodes of one short task, about {{tel_ep_s}} s each, where the papers use hours of varied data; HWM, for instance, uses about 130 h against our 0.6 h. On a short task with one arm, the commanded joints already hold most of the state, which leaves little for a latent to add (class C). The task is also easy for our policy, since SmolVLA is at its ceiling on it, so there is little uncertainty for an entropy rule to read: the chunk length chosen by adaptive chunking sat at its minimum in 86 % of decisions. For H-JEPA, our task lies outside the regime the paper targets, as there is no gap between slow and fast parts of the scene.

We also have too few failures. Our policies mostly succeed, and the main rollout set has only {{ro_p_fail}} failures in {{ro_p_eps}} episodes. A bar such as five failures caught out of eight is coarse, and a single episode can change the verdict (ReAP, VLA-Corrector, SURVEYOR).

In several cases our version of the method is not exactly theirs. For ContactGuard we used mixed policies and two cameras, where the paper uses one policy and three cameras. For FAIL-Detect and SAFE we used our world model's surprise as the score instead of their own scores. Our VLA-Corrector verifier works on pooled image statistics, not on their 54 features with the arm state. SURVEYOR's drafter is not released, REMORA's progress atoms are written by hand, and Robometer was only tested zero-shot because our fine-tuning run never trained.

Episode length is strong because of how the episodes end, not because judges are weak in general. On ArmnetBench a failed episode tends to run until the time limit, so length separates the outcomes ({{ex_b_len}}); on our own 88 rollouts, where this is not the case, length is close to chance ({{b_len_own}}).

Our inference engine matters as well. vla.cpp samples sequentially and has no batching, so adaptive chunking pays for 20 full passes, while the paper's 106 ms was measured with batching on an A800. For PACT-VLA, the time of the check is projected rather than measured. Finally, several tests were offline only: planning ranked recorded chunks, and chunking measured smoothness on recorded episodes. None of these ran in closed loop on the arm.

Table 4: Each fail or not-proven paper, what we measured, and what on our side may explain it.

| failed paper | kind | our result | what may be on our side |
|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | world model | planning rank 0.60, action-only 0.68–0.90 | one short task, 157 episodes, one seed; offline ranking only |
| DINO-WM | world model | planning rank 0.61, action-only 0.68–0.90 | same data as LeWorldModel; one training |
| RoboJEPA | world model | 11.8 % below copy-last, bar 15 % | near miss; the 1B model reached 15.8 % on 30 clips only |
| H-JEPA | world model | progress ρ −0.73, bar −0.80; false stalls 52 % | our task is outside the paper's regime |
| HWM | world model | not proven: high level worse at 2 s | 0.6 h of one task against their 130 h |
| ContactGuard | monitor | AUROC 0.70, current latent 0.82 | mixed policies and 2 cameras against one policy and 3 cameras |
| FAIL-Detect and SAFE | monitor | 22.7 % of successes flagged at α 5 % | our score, not theirs; 22 successes for calibration |
| ReAP | monitor | 3 of 8 failures caught | 8 failures only |
| VLA-Corrector | monitor | 2 of 8, AUROC 0.59 | our features, not their 54; 8 failures |
| Robometer | judge | AUROC 0.818, length 0.938 | zero-shot only; ArmnetBench failures run to the time limit (on our rollouts length is near chance) |
| SURVEYOR | monitor | threshold 1.249 against 0.20; 3 of 8 | their drafter not released; 8 failures |
| REMORA | monitor | readout flips 0.70 of the per-frame readout | hand-written progress atoms |
| Adaptive chunking | chunking | 4,751 ms per decision; length 2 in transport and at the grasp | sequential engine, no batching; task at ceiling for our policy |
| PACT-VLA | chunking | 37 ticks of delay against 8 | check time projected; sequential engine |
| Real-Time Chunking | chunking | not proven: 71–73 % smoother, no arm run | offline smoothness only |
| Horizon × depth | chunking | not proven: 0.78, bar 0.70 | one policy, one task; arm test not run |

## 8. Limitations

Our data covers one short task family, about {{tel_ep_s}} s per episode, with one arm, two cameras and about {{tel_min}} minutes of demonstrations, so it says nothing about long-horizon tasks or varied scenes. There are few failures to test the monitors on, {{ro_p_fail}} in the main set of {{ro_p_eps}}. Several methods are our own re-implementations rather than the authors' code end to end (Table 4), and the planning and chunking tests ran offline, never in closed loop on the arm. A single operator ran the arm and labelled the outcomes without being blind to them, and many tests use small samples and a single training seed. The VLA policies have no cheap baseline, only a success rate over 10 trials, which is a weaker test than the others. We chose the papers from the stack we run, so the rates describe these papers and not the field. Finally, a failure on our rig is not a failure on the authors' hardware, and our own mistakes are possible, as the TurboVLA case shows (Section 3).

## 9. Related work

Two works evaluate policies on the same arm: a real-world VLA benchmark with a taxonomy of failures and recoveries [@2606.08881], and ArmnetBench, a fleet of SO-101 cells that evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. In simulation, vla-eval runs VLAs across 14 benchmarks [@2603.13966], and another benchmark studies the quality of demonstrations [@2610.18869]. These works rank policies. We instead take the claim a paper makes about its method and test it against a bar fixed in advance, on one cheap rig. The closest work is RoboSeed [@2610.40026], which scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability; its authors note that the score is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one yes or no per claim on one rig, with the data behind it.

## 10. Conclusion

We re-tested {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a rig of about €{{rig_eur_round}}, with under ${{cost_round}} of cloud GPU, and {{c_paper_repro}} of them held. Several methods lost to a baseline with no learning in it: copy-last, the commanded joints or the length of the episode. Our advice to anyone re-testing a paper on a cheap rig is to run the cheapest baseline first, on the same data, with the bar fixed before the test. Everything is public, and every number can be rebuilt from the repository. Authors who think a failure here is ours (Table 4) are welcome to tell us, and anyone with a cheap rig can run the same tests.

